---
metadata: {
    partial_name: "Sign In | Check Link",
}
---
{%- comment -%}
  Checks an emailed sign-in link. Called with:
    function link = 'account/sign_in/check_link', email: email, token: token, nonce: nonce
  Returns { "ok": true|false, "reason": "stale"|"expired", "request_id": ..., "user_id": ..., "return_to": ... }.
  ok only when all four hold:
    1. The nonce belongs to a pending sign_in_link request
    2. That request was made for this email
    3. The request has not expired
    4. The platform accepts the token as live for this email's account
  When ok is false, reason says why, and the caller sends it as error=<reason>:
    stale    1 or 2 failed: no such request, one already used, or one made for another address.
             Nothing here belongs to the person asking, so it says nothing more.
    expired  1 and 2 held, so the link is a real one that was sent to this address, but it is
             no longer valid: its 15 minutes have passed (3), or the platform no longer accepts
             its token (4). The platform ends a token when the account's password changes, so a
             link that was sent before a password reset lands here.
  The reason depends only on the link itself. It does not depend on whether an account exists
  for the address, and a request for an unknown address stores no pending request to match, so
  nothing can tell the two apart. Checking changes nothing. The caller uses the link with
  take_slot, so it works once.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign link = {}
  assign link['ok'] = false
  assign link['reason'] = 'stale'

  assign email = email | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase
  assign token = token | default: '' | append: ''
  assign nonce = nonce | default: '' | append: ''
  if email == blank or token == blank or nonce == blank
    return link
  endif

  graphql found = 'account/sign_in/check_link', nonce: nonce, email: email, token: token
  assign request = found.requests.results | first
  if request.id == blank or request.email != email
    return link
  endif
  if found.user.id == blank
    return link
  endif

  comment
    From here the request is a real, pending one for this address, so a failure means the link
    is no longer valid, not that it is unknown.
  endcomment
  assign link['reason'] = 'expired'
  assign now_unix = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign expires_unix = request.expires_at | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  if expires_unix <= now_unix
    return link
  endif
  if found.user.authenticate.temporary_token != true
    return link
  endif

  assign link['reason'] = ''

  assign link['ok'] = true
  assign link['request_id'] = request.id
  assign link['user_id'] = found.user.id
  assign link['return_to'] = request.return_to
  return link
-%}
